# Story: on a Mac as it comes, with macOS's own tiling on, someone flicks a docked window upwards, or carries it to where macOS tiles.
# Arbor stands aside there and sets no frame while macOS may be moving the window (issue #3, rule 1; QUESTIONS Q23), so the window leaves its
# slot as large as the slot. That size is never taken for its own (rule 5; board 1.67), and if macOS does not take the window it has its
# own size half a second later, where it lies (board 1.56).
# The simulated display is 1440x900 with a 25-point menu bar.
quiet
open Safari
open Notes
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
wait 1
expect size 101 464x851
# Thrown into the menu bar and let go at once: macOS fills the screen only for a pointer that has rested there, so it does nothing.
drag 101 700 10 quick
expect floating 101
expect size 101 464x851
expect moved-once
wait 1
expect size 101 640x420
expect visible 101
expect moved-once
float-all
wait 1
expect size 101 640x420
# A way out taken within that half second goes by the books: Float All, a pause, Quit.
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
wait 1
drag 101 700 10 quick
float-all
expect size 101 640x420
wait 1
expect size 101 640x420
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
wait 1
drag 101 700 10 quick
pause
expect size 101 640x420
resume
wait 1
expect size 101 640x420
# Rested in the menu bar, macOS fills the screen with it: that is what the hand asked macOS for, and Arbor never touches it.
dock 101 left
wait 1
expect size 101 464x851
drag 101 700 0
wait 1
expect floating 101
expect size 101 1440x875
# Dragged off that tile, macOS gives it the size it had when macOS took it, which was the slot's. Let go, it has its own.
drag 101 700 300
wait 1
expect floating 101
expect size 101 640x420
dock 101 left
wait 1
drag 101 700 10 quick
quit-check
expect replied QUIT ok
expect size 101 640x420
expect size 102 640x420
